<crash-analysis>
**Analysis:**
The crash is a memory leak detected by LeakSanitizer. The leak originates in the `tidy-html5` library's `prvTidynewStack` function, which allocates nodes for an internal stack (`lexer->istack`) used during parsing.

The root cause is an incomplete cleanup routine in the library. The function `prvTidyFreeLexer`, which is supposed to release all memory associated with the lexer during the `tidyRelease` process, fails to deallocate the nodes remaining in the `lexer->istack` linked list. While other parts of the code may pop nodes from this stack during parsing, any nodes left on the stack when parsing finishes are never freed, leading to the reported memory leak.

The fuzz driver is not at fault; it correctly follows the library's API by pairing `tidyCreate()` with a corresponding `tidyRelease()` call.

**Suggestion for Patching the Project:**
The bug can be fixed by adding logic to `prvTidyFreeLexer` in `/src/tidy-html5/src/lexer.c` to drain and free any remaining nodes on the `lexer->istack`.

A loop should be added at the beginning of `prvTidyFreeLexer` to call `prvTidyPopInline` until the stack is empty.

**Example Patch:**
```c
/* In /src/tidy-html5/src/lexer.c */
void prvTidyFreeLexer( TidyDocImpl* doc )
{
Lexer* lexer;

if ( !doc || !doc->lexer )
return;

lexer = doc->lexer;

/* Add this loop to free any remaining nodes on the inline stack. */
while ( lexer->istack )
{
prvTidyPopInline( doc, NULL );
}

if ( lexer->pushed.size > 0 )
TY_(free)( doc->allocator, lexer->pushed.stack );

TY_(free)( doc->allocator, lexer->lexbuf );
TY_(free)( doc->allocator, lexer->token );

TY_(free)( doc->allocator, lexer );
doc->lexer = NULL;
}
```
</crash-analysis>

